RULES AND REGULATIONS TO IMPLEMENT THE INTENT AND PROVISIONS OF SECTION 4 (h) OF PRESIDENTIAL DECREE NO. 538, AS AMENDED

Pursuant to paragraph (h) of Section 4 of Presidential Decree No. 538, as amended, the Board of Directors of the PHIVIDEC Industrial Authority through Board through Resolution No. VII-832, Series of 2008 hereby prescribed and promulgated the following Rules and Regulations for the guidance and compliance of all concerned:

CHAPTER 1

General Provisions

SECTION 1. Title. — This Code shall be named Implementing Rules and Regulations in Levying, Assessing, and Collecting Real Property Tax.

SECTION 2. Scope. — This Title shall govern the administration, tax mapping, appraisal, assessment, levy, and collection of real property tax inside PIA's proclaimed Industrial Estate. (Sec. 4/h, P.D. 538 & Sec. 197 of R.A. 7160). ISCDEA

SECTION 3. Fundamental Principles. — The appraisal, assessment, levy, and collection of real property tax shall be guided by the following fundamental principles (Sec. 198, R.A. 7160):

a) Real property shall be appraised at its current and fair market value;

b) Real property shall be classified for assessment purposes on the basis of its actual use;

c) Real property shall be assessed on the basis of a uniform classification within the Industrial Estate of PIA;

d) The appraisal, assessment, levy, and collection of real property tax shall not be let to any private person; and HTacDS

e) The appraisal and assessment of real property shall be equitable.

SECTION 4. Real Property Subject to Tax. — (As defined in Art. 415 of the Civil Code) Permanent or Immovable Property:

(1) Land, buildings, roads, and constructions of all kinds adhered to the soil;

(2) Trees, plants and growing fruits while they are attached to the land or form an integral part of an immovable;

(3) Everything attached to an immovable in a fixed manner in such a way that it cannot be separated from without breaking the material or deterioration of the object;

(4) Statues, reliefs, paintings, or other objects for use or ornamentation placed in buildings or on lands by the owner of the immovable in such a manner that it reveals the intention to attach them permanently to the tenements;

Machinery, receptacles, instruments, or implements intended by the owner of the tenement for an industry or works which may be carried on in a building or on a piece of land and which tend directly to meet the needs of the said industry or works; aHCSTD

(5) Animal houses, pigeon houses, beehives, fishponds or breeding places of similar nature in case their owner has placed them or preserved them with the intention to have them permanently attached to the land and forming a permanent part of it; the animals in these places are included;

(6) Mines, quarries and slag dumps while the matter thereof forms part of the bed and waters either running or stagnant;

(7) Docks and structures which though floating are intended by their nature and object to remain at a fixed place on a river, lake or coast; and

(8) Contracts for public works and servitudes and other real rights over immovable property.

SECTION 5. Definition of Terms. —

(a) "Acquisition Cost" for newly-acquired locally made machinery, not yet depreciated and appraised within the year of its purchase, refers to the actual cost of the machinery to its present owner, plus the cost of transportation, handling and installation at the present site. For imported machinery, the add-on cost includes freight & insurance charges, bank charges, brokerage, arrastre, customs duties and taxes or equivalent to 15-20% depending on its weight and volume, of its purchased value. DcSEHT

(b) "Actual Use" refers to the purpose for which the property is principally or predominantly utilized by the person in possession thereof;

(c) "Ad Valorem Tax" is a levy on real property determined on the basis of a fixed proportion of the value of the property;

(d) "Agricultural Land" is a land devoted principally to the planting of trees, raising crops, livestock and poultry, dairying, and similar aquaculture activities, and other agricultural activities, and is not classified as mineral, timber, residential, commercial or industrial land;

(e) "Appraisal" is the act or process of determining the value of property as of a specific date for a specific purpose;

(f) "Assessment" is the act or process of determining the value of property, or proportion thereof subject to tax, including the discovery in mapping operation, listing, classification, and appraisal;

(g) "Assessment Level" is the percentage applied to the fair market value to determine the taxable value of the property;

(h) "Assessed Value" is the fair market value after deducting the cost of depreciation of the real property multiplied by the assessment level. It is synonymous to taxable value; cEISAD

(i) "Commercial Land" is a land devoted principally for the object of profit and is not classified as agricultural, industrial, mineral, timber, or residential land;

(j) "Depreciated Value" is the value remaining after deducting depreciation from the acquisition cost or reproduction or replacement cost.

(k) "Economic Life" is the estimated period over which it is anticipated that a building or any civil structure, machinery or equipment may be profitably utilized;

(l) "Fair Market Value" is the price at which a property may be sold by a seller who is not compelled to sell and bought by a buyer who is not compelled to buy;

(m) "Improvement" is a valuable addition made to a real property or an amelioration in its condition, amounting to more than a mere repair or replacement of parts involving capital expenditures and labour, which is intended to enhance its value, beauty or utility or to adapt it for new or further purposes;

(n) "Industrial Land" is a land devoted principally to industrial activity as capital investment and is not classified as agricultural, commercial, timber, mineral, or residential land; DHAcET

(o) "Machinery" embraces machines, equipment, mechanical contrivances, instruments, appliances or apparatus which may or may not be attached permanently or temporarily to the real property. It includes the physical facilities for production, the installation and appurtenant service facilities, those which are mobile, self-powered or self-propelled, and those not permanently attached to the real property which are actually, directly, and exclusively used to meet the needs of the particular industry, business or activity and which by their very nature and purpose are designed for, or necessary to its manufacturing, mining, logging, commercial, industrial or agricultural purposes. It also includes machines, equipment, mechanical contrivances, instruments, appliances & apparatus, which are mobile, self-propelled, operating on wheels or floaters, such as cars, trucks, airplanes, tractors, bulldozers, graders, loaders, yarders, cranes, trains, ships, boats, and/or similar kinds, essential to meet the needs of a particular industry, business and works are considered real property for taxation purposes. (Rule 2, Assessment Regulation No. 2-79).

(p) "Mineral Lands" are lands in which minerals, metallic or non-metallic, exist in sufficient quantity or grade to justify the necessary expenditures to extract and utilize such materials;

(q) "Reassessment" is the assigning of new assessed values to property, particularly real estate, as the result of a general, partial, or individual reappraisal of the property;

(r) "Remaining Economic Life" is the period of times expressed in years from the date of appraisal to the date when the property (machine & building) becomes valueless;

(s) "Remaining Value" is the value corresponding to the remaining useful life of the property (machine & building); TEaADS

(t) "Replacement or Reproduction Cost" is the cost that would be incurred on the basis of current prices, in acquiring an equally desirable substitute property, or the cost of reproducing a new replica of the property on the basis of current prices with the same or closely similar material;

(u) "Residential Land" is land principally devoted to habitation.

CHAPTER 2

Appraisal and Assessment of Real Property

SECTION 6. Appraisal of Real Property. — All real properties located in the PHIVIDEC Industrial Estate-Misamis Oriental (PIE-MO), whether taxable or exempt shall be appraised at the current and fair market value prevailing in the locality where the property is situated. (Sec. 201)

SECTION 7. Declaration of Real Property by the Owner or Administrator. — (a) It shall be the duty of all persons, natural or juridical, owning or administering real property within the declared PIE-MO area and/or had already been acquired by and within the area of the Authority, including the improvements therein, or their duly authorized representative, to prepare or cause to be prepared and file with the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division, a sworn statement declaring the true value of their property, whether previously declared or undeclared, taxable or exempt, which shall be the current and fair market value of the property, as determined by the declarant. (b) Such declaration shall contain a description of the property sufficient in detail to enable the Estate Assessor/Chief, Rev. Assessment Division or his deputy to identify the same for assessment purposes. (c) All property owners or administrators or their duly authorized representatives shall file the sworn declaration of real property herein referred to shall be filed with the office of the Estate Assessor/Revenue Assessment Division once every three (3) years during the period from January first (1st) to June thirtieth (30th) commencing with the calendar year 1992. (d) The PIA shall prescribe a standard form to be known as Sworn Declaration of Property Values for the use of all PIA assessors' offices (Sec. 202). CEDScA

SECTION 8. Duty of Person Acquiring Real Property Improvements or Making Improvement Thereon. — It shall also be the duty of any person, or his authorized representative acquiring at any time real property in the PHIVIDEC Industrial Estate or making any improvement on the real property already acquired by, sold to or leased by the Authority to prepare, or cause to be prepared, and file with the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division, a sworn statement declaring the true value of the subject property within sixty (60) days after the acquisition of such property or upon completion of construction or upon occupancy of the improvement, whichever comes earlier. (Sec. 203).

For purposes of establishing ownership, date of acquisition/installation and value of the property the following are required to submit to the PIA Assessor:

(b) For residential houses, building, or other improvements acquired or newly constructed which will require building permits, property owners or their authorized representatives shall likewise file a sworn declaration of the true value of the subject house, building, or other improvements within sixty (60) days after its acquisition or construction.

(1) The date of a duly notarised deeds of sale, contract, or other deed of conveyance covering the subject property executed between the contracting parties;

(2) The date of completion or occupancy of the newly constructed building, house, or improvements whichever comes earlier; CAIaDT

(3) The date of completion or occupancy of any expansion, renovation, additional structures or improvements made upon any existing building, house, or other real property, whichever comes earlier.

(4) For machinery, the sixty-day period for filling the required sworn declaration of property value shall commence on the date of installation thereof as determined by the Estate Assessor/Chief Revenue Assessment Division and for this purpose, said Assessor may secure certification from the building official or municipal engineer or other appropriate official stationed in the province, city, or municipality concerned. AEIcSa

The Board of Directors of PIA shall pass a resolution providing the imposition of penalties or pecuniary fines on property owners or administrators who fail to comply with this Article.

SECTION 9. Declaration of Real Property by the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division. — (a) When any person, natural or juridical by whom real property is required to be declared under Section 8 hereof, refuses or fails for any reason to make such declaration within the time prescribed, the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division shall himself declare the property in the name of the defaulting owner, if known or against an unknown owner, as the case may be and shall assess the property for taxation purposes in accordance with the provision of this Title. Provided, that such property is constructed or installed on PIA acquired land as indicated in Section 4 (h) of P.D. 538. No oath shall be required of a declaration thus made by the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Div. or his deputy. (Sec. 204)

(b) The PIA may adapt a standard form of the sworn Declaration of Property Values for the use of PIA Assessor, as well as the procedures to be observed in the filling and safekeeping.

The PIA Board of Directors may enact a resolution providing for the imposition of penalties or pecuniary fines on property owners or administrators who fail to comply with this Article.

SECTION 10. Tax Mapping Control Roll. — a) There shall be prepared and maintained by the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Div. a Tax Map Control Roll wherein all real properties mapped shall be listed, whether taxable or exempt, the year acquired, constructed or installed, the acquisition cost, location number, the percentage of yearly depreciation, and the property index number (PIN). b) All real property listed must have the corresponding Tax Declaration, whether taxable or exempt, duly signed by the Estate Assessor/Chief, and the Deputy Assessor of the Revenue Assessment Div.

SECTION 11. Listing of Real Property in the Assessment Rolls. — (a) There shall be prepared and maintained by the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division an assessment roll wherein all real property shall be listed, whether taxable or exempt, located on land which had already been acquired by and within the jurisdiction of the Authority. These properties shall be listed, valued and assessed in the name of the PHIVIDEC Industrial Authority. (b) The real property owned by the Republic of the Philippines, its instrumentalities and political subdivisions, the beneficial use of which has been granted, for consideration or otherwise, to a taxable person, shall be listed, valued and assessed in the name of the possessor, grantee or of the public entity if such property has been acquired or held for resale or lease. (c) The real property of a corporation, partnership, already purchased by PIA or association shall be listed, valued and assessed in the same manner as that of an individual. (Sec. 204)

SECTION 12. Proof of Exemption of Real Property from Taxation. — Every person by or for whom real property is declared, who shall claim tax exemption for such property under this Implementing Rules and Regulations shall file with the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division within thirty (30) days from the date of the declaration of real property with sufficient documentary evidence in support of such claim including corporate charters, title of ownership, articles of incorporation, by laws, contracts, affidavits, certification and mortgage deeds, and similar documents. EITcaD

If the required evidence is not submitted within the period herein prescribed, the property shall be listed as taxable in the assessment roll. However, if the property shall be proven to be tax exempt, the same shall be dropped from the assessment roll. (Sec. 206)

SECTION 13. Real Property Identification System. — All declarations of real property made under the provisions of this Implementing Rules and Regulations shall be kept and filed under a uniform classification system to be established by the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division and be designated with permanent property index number. (Sec. 207)

SECTION 14. Notification of Transfer of Real Property Ownership. — (a) Any person who shall transfer real property ownership to another shall notify the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division, within sixty (60) days from the date of such transfer. The notification shall include the mode of transfer, the description of the property alienated, the name and address of the transferee and any related document(s). (Sec. 208)

(b)In addition to the notice of transfer, the previous property owner shall likewise surrender to the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division (RAD) concerned, the tax declaration covering the subject property in order that the same may be cancelled from the assessment records of the RAD. If, however said previous owner still owns property other than the property alienated, he/she shall, within the prescribed sixty-day period, file with the Estate Assessor concerned in accordance with the Provisions of Section 8 and Section 9 of this rule.

SECTION 15. Duty of the Port Revenue Division, PIA. — It is the duty of the Chief, Port Revenue Division to furnish accomplished copy of BOC (Bu. Of Customs) Import Entry & Internal Revenue Declaration form for machinery, equipment mechanical contrivances, instruments, appliances, & other paraphernalia, or all machinery defined in Section 5 (o) of this Implementing Rules and Regulations, to the Estate Assessor Chief, Revenue Assessment Division. SECHIA

SECTION 16. Duty of the Provincial Register of Deeds to Appraise Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division of Real Property within the PIA Industrial Estate Listed in the Registry. — (a) To ascertain whether or not any real property in the Estate entered in the Registry of Property has escaped discovery and listing for the purpose of taxation, the Provincial Register of Deeds upon prior coordination with the PIA shall prepare and submit to the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division within six (6) months from the date of effectivity of registration every year thereafter, an abstract of his registry, which shall include brief but sufficient description of the real properties entered therein, their present owners, and the dates of their most recent transfer or alienation accompanied by copies of corresponding deeds of sale, donation or partition or other forms of alienation, (b) Upon prior coordination the Provincial Register of Deeds to require every person who shall present for registration a document of transfer, alienation, or encumbrance of real property to accompany the same with a certificate to the effect that the real property subject of the transfer, alienation, or encumbrance, as the case may be, has been fully paid of all real property taxes due thereon and shall forward the same to PIA office. Failure to provide such certificate shall be a valid cause for the Registrar of Deeds to refuse the registration of the document.

SECTION 17. Duty of Official Issuing Building Permit or Certificate of Registration of Machinery to Transmit Copy to PIA Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division. — Any public official or employee from PIA or other government agencies who may now or hereafter be required by law or regulation to issue to any person a permit for the construction, addition, repair, or renovation of a building, or permanent improvement on land, or a certificate of registration for any machinery, recondition or renovation for any machinery, including machines, mechanical contrivances and apparatus attached or affixed on land inside the proclaimed area and owned by the Authority, shall transmit a copy of such permit or certificate within thirty (30) days of its issuance to the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division where the property is situated. (Sec. 210)

Where it is found to be more convenient or efficient, said official may, in lieu of individual copies of the said permits or certificates within the first ten (10) days of the succeeding month.

(b) Any official referred to in paragraph (a) hereof shall likewise furnish the Estate Assessor concerned copies of the building floor plans and/or certificates of registration or installation of other machineries which may not be permanently or temporarily attached to land or another real property but falling under the definition of the term machinery under Article 291 of this Rule and the rules and guidelines issued by DOF. aACHDS

SECTION 18. Duty of Geodetic Engineers to Furnish Copy of Plans to Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division. — Upon prior coordination with concerned authorities such as the Land Management Bureau, Land Registration Authority, it shall be the duty of all Geodetic Engineers, public or private, to furnish free of charge to the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division all lands within the Estate, a white or blue print copy of each of all approved original or subdivision plans or maps of surveys executed by them within thirty (30) days from receipt of such plans from the Land Management Bureau, the Land Registration Authority, as the case may be. (Sec. 211)

SECTION 19. Preparation of Schedule of Fair Market Values. — Before any general revision of property assessment is made, there shall be prepared a schedule of fair market values of real properties by the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division or the PIA may adapt the Schedule of Values approved by the Sangguniang Panlalawigan to avoid disparity of values. Such Schedule of Values shall also be enacted by a resolution by the PIA Board of Directors. The approved schedule of fair market values shall be posted in the PIA Administration Building and two other conspicuous public places therein. (Sec. 212)

SECTION 20. Authority of the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Div. to Take Evidence. — For the purpose of obtaining information on which to base the market value of any real property, the Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Division or his deputy may summon the owner of the property to be affected or person having legal interest therein and witnesses, administer oaths, and take deposition concerning the property, its ownership, amount, nature, and value. (Sec. 213)

SECTION 21. Amendment of Schedule of Fair Market Values. — The Estate Assessor/Chief, Revenue Assessment Div. through the Manager, Estate Management Department may recommend to the PIA Board of Directors amendments to correct errors in valuation of the schedule of fair market values. The Board shall, by resolution, act upon the recommendation within ninety (90) days from receipt thereof. (Sec. 214)

SECTION 22. Classes of Real Property for Assessment Purposes. — For purposes of assessment, all properties in PIE-MO has been classified as Industrial as provided for in P.D. 1491. The PIA Management shall have the power to classify lands in the Estate in accordance with the established land use and zoning policy. (Sec. 215) aSTHDc

SECTION 23. Special Classes of Real Property. — All lands, buildings and other improvements thereon actually, directly and exclusively used for hospitals, cultural or scientific purposes, and those owned and used by local water district, and government-owned or controlled corporations rendering essential public services in the supply and distribution of water and/or generation and transmission of electric power shall be classified as special. (Sec. 216)

SECTION 24. Actual Use of Real Property as Basis for Assessment. — Real property located within the PHIVIDEC Industrial Estate-Misamis Oriental shall be classified as industrial. PIA owned land which has not yet been leased to industries shall be valued and assessed as industrial. Likewise, properties which are used as resettlement sites, commercial, residential, etc. shall be assessed on the basis of its actual use. (Sec. 217) (P.D. No. 1961)

